.TH PWD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pwd \- print the name of the current working directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pwd
.RB [ \-L | \-P ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pwd
writes the absolute path name of the current working directory,
followed by a newline, to standard output.
.PP
In logical mode (the default), the value of the
.B PWD
environment variable is printed if it is an absolute path name that
contains no
.B .\&
or
.B ..\&
components and names the same directory (same device and inode) as
the current directory.
Otherwise, and always in physical mode,
.B pwd
prints the path returned by
.BR getcwd (3),
which contains no symbolic links.
Path names longer than
.B PATH_MAX
are handled by retrying with a larger buffer, up to about 1\~MiB.
.PP
Scanning for options stops at
.BR \-\- ,
at a lone
.BR \- ,
or at the first argument that does not begin with
.BR \- .
Operands are ignored.
.PP
The shell usually provides its own built-in
.BR pwd ;
this page describes the stand-alone program.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-L
Logical mode, as described above.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-P
Physical mode: always use
.BR getcwd (3).
.PP
If both are given, the last one wins.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The current directory could not be determined, or writing to standard
output failed.
.TP
.B 2
An invalid option was given.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PWD
Used in logical mode, subject to the checks described above.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR readlink (1),
.BR getcwd (3)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2024.
